1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Ndf3 b6 8. Bb5 Qc7 9. Ne2 Be7 10. O-O Bb7 11. Be3 (11. f5 {would be very interesting, e.g.} exf5 12. Nf4 Nd8 13. e6 Nxe6 14. Nxe6 fxe6 15. Ne5 Bc8 {and now, the best move in chess,} 16. Qh5+ {(Bronstein)}) 11... h6 (11... g6) 12. Ng3 (12. f5 exf5 13. Nf4 Nd8 14. e6 {Barden}) 12... g6 13. b3 a6 14. Bd3 Na5 15. Bc2 h5 16. Nh1 Rc8 17. Nf2 Nf8 18. Rc1 cxd4 19. Bxd4 Nc6 20. Bd3 Nd7 21. Qd2 Nc5 22. Bb1 Na5 23. Ng5 Naxb3 24. axb3 Nxb3 25. Qe3 Nxc1 26. Rxc1 Bc5 27. Nf3 a5 28. Bc2 Ba6 29. Nh3 b5 30. Bd3 Qb6 31. Nhg5 a4 32. Ra1 Ke7 33. Kf2 Bxd4 34. Qxd4 Qxd4+ 35. Nxd4 Rxc3 36. Bxb5 Bxb5 37. Nxb5 Rc2+ 38. Kg3 Rb8 39. Nd4 Rc4 40. Ngf3 Rbb4 {"The manoeuvre from New York 1927," commented Bronstein. [Ref. to Capa-Nimzo]} 41. Ne2 Re4 42. Nc3 Rxf4 43. Na2 Rg4+ 44. Kh3 Rbc4 45. Rb1 Rc2 46. Rb7+ Kf8 47. Nb4 Rcxg2 {Sources: 'Nigel Short: Chess Prodigy' by David Short (Faber & Faber, 1981), p71; Manchester Evening News, 24 January 1976; The Guardian, 26 January 1976; The Times, 7 February 1976; Daily Telegraph, 7 February 1976; BCM, February 1976, p66. Korchnoi's score: +20, =9, -1. Drew with HD MacPherson, John Burke, CA Plasa, Simon JB Knott, Geoff W Lawton, C Sonabend, William N Watson, Clive A Frostick, AD Gregory.} 0-1

