1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 c5 3. d5 e6 4. e4 exd5 5. exd5 $2 (5. e5 d4 (5... Ng8) 6. exf6 dxc3 7. Qe2+ $18) 5... d6 6. Nf3 a6 7. a4 Bg4 8. Be2 Bxf3 9. Bxf3 Nbd7 10. O-O Be7 11. Bf4 O-O 12. Qd2 Re8 13. Rfe1 Nf8 {(Ng6, Nd7)} 14. Ne4 Nxe4 15. Bxe4 Bf6 16. c3 Qd7 17. Qd3 Qc7 18. Bf5 g6 19. Bh3 Re7 20. Rxe7 Qxe7 21. Kf1 Re8 22. Qd2 $13 Qe4 $2 23. Bxd6 Qc4+ 24. Kg1 Re2 25. Qf4 $1 Qxf4 26. Bxf4 Rxb2 27. d6 {"Black now is forced to defend passively. I wasn’t sure whether I was really winning, so I offered Mr Eley a draw which would make me first in the tournament — but he declined it." (Short)} Ne6 28. Bxe6 fxe6 29. Rd1 Kf7 30. d7 Bd8 31. h4 $18 h6 32. Bxh6 b5 33. Bg5 Ba5 34. axb5 axb5 35. d8=Q Bxd8 36. Rxd8 b4 37. cxb4 cxb4 38. Rb8 e5 39. g4 b3 40. h5 gxh5 41. gxh5 e4 42. Rb7+ {Sources: 'Nigel Short: Chess Prodigy' by David Short (Faber & Faber, 1981), p101; Chess Player No.15, 1977B/327 (notes by Short); The Guardian, 4 June 1977} 1-0

