1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nge2 dxe4 5. a3 Be7 6. g3 f5 7. Bg2 c5 $2 {This move seemed a good idea at the time but really I should have played Nf6 and castled before attempting to grab some more central control. After all, I was playing the Black pieces and should have been looking more to consolidating and not losing ground, rather than getting too adventurous too soon. I paid for it later - I lost because my king was stuck in the middle. Still, lessons learned the hard way certainly stick in the mind and since this game I have always been mindful of the advisability of tucking the king away as soon as is convenient, and certainly before embarking upon any premature advances - Short, "Nigel Short's Chess Skills"} 8. Bf4 Nc6 9. d5 Nd4 10. Nxd4 cxd4 11. Qxd4 Bf6 12. Qc4 Bxc3+ 13. Qxc3 Nf6 14. dxe6 Bxe6 15. Rd1 Qe7 16. Bd6 Qf7 17. O-O Rc8 18. Qd4 b6 19. f3 Rc4 20. Qd2 Qb7 21. b3 Rc8 22. fxe4 Nxe4 23. Bxe4 fxe4 24. Bb4 Bh3 25. Qd6 {Source: 'Nigel Short: Chess Prodigy' by David Short (Faber & Faber, 1981), p109} 1-0

