1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Qb6 4. Bxc6 {"Neither Fuller, myself, or anyone else watching the game had seen this before. It looks so silly - sure, White gains a developing move, but he has conceded the two bishops and Black has no real weaknesses. As it turned out, though, the extra development paid off." (Murray Chandler, The Listener (NZ), 5 August 1977)} Qxc6 $2 (4... bxc6 {Short}) 5. O-O g6 $6 {Chandler} (5... d6) (5... e6) 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. d4 d6 8. dxc5 dxc5 9. Bf4 $1 {Chandler, Short} (9. Nd5 $14 {Stockfish}) 9... Bxc3 10. bxc3 Qxe4 11. Qc1 $1 Bf5 (11... f6 $15 {Stockfish}) 12. Re1 Qa4 (12... Qxc2 13. Qa3 b6 14. Rad1 {Rd2 Short}) 13. Qe3 {(Be5xf6 Short)} Qc6 (13... e6 14. Be5 f6 15. Qxc5 {Short}) 14. Ne5 (14. Bh6 $3 $18 {Stockfish}) 14... Qc8 15. Nc4 Be6 (15... Qe6 16. Nd6+ {Short}) 16. Qe5 Nf6 17. Nd6+ exd6 18. Qxf6 Rg8 (18... Kd7 19. Rad1 {Short}) 19. Bxd6 Qd7 (19... Qd8 20. Rxe6+ {Short}) 20. Rad1 {( Bxc5 Short) Sources: 'Nigel Short: Chess Prodigy' by David Short (Faber & Faber, 1981), p118; The Chess Player No.15, 1977B/383 (notes by Short); Manchester Evening News, 25 June 1977; The Guardian, 2 July 1977 (Leonard Barden); The Listener (NZ), 5 August 1977 (Murray Chandler)} 1-0

