{Time limit 40 moves in 2hrs} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Nb3 Bb6 6. c4 {A strong line for White} d6 7. Nc3 a6 {Else 8.b5 is a bit awkward.} 8. g3 Nc6 9. Bg2 Ne5 {A bit of tactical play to catch up on development.} 10. Qe2 Qc7 11. Nd2 Ne7 12. f4 N5c6 13. Nf3 Nd4 14. Nxd4 Bxd4 15. Bd2 Bd7 16. Rc1 Rc8 17. b3 Qb6 {I felt I was holding my own.} 18. Qf3 Bc6 19. Ne2 {[25']} Bc5 {[53']} 20. g4 {I felt that it was now fairly critical and that I had to react actively or get squashed so......} f5 21. gxf5 exf5 22. Nc3 ({This surprised me. All my calculations had been based on} 22. Qh5+ g6 23. Qh6 Bb4 {etc.}) 22... Bb4 23. Nd5 Bxd2+ 24. Kxd2 Qd4+ ({If} 24... Qa5+ 25. Qc3 Qxa2+ $2 26. Rc2) 25. Qd3 {Best} Qf2+ 26. Qe2 Qxe2+ ({If} 26... Qd4+ 27. Ke1) 27. Kxe2 fxe4 28. Bxe4 Nxd5 29. cxd5 Bb5+ {[1hr 31']} 30. Kf3 {[37']} Rxc1 31. Rxc1 Kd8 32. Bf5 Bd7 33. Be6 Rf8 34. Ke4 Rf6 35. f5 g6 36. Bxd7 Kxd7 37. fxg6 Rxg6 38. Rf1 {[46']} Ke7 ({[1hr 47'] The position should be drawn but I eventually lose due to not playing accurately enough. I decided to try to hold it together whereas I should have activated the Rook. Probably} 38... Rg4+ {is best.}) 39. Rf4 Rg2 40. Rh4 Rg7 ({Again, maybe} 40... Rxa2 41. Rxh7+ Kf6 {is best.}) 41. a4 Rf7 42. a5 Kd7 43. Kd4 Re7 44. Kc3 Kc7 45. b4 b5 {It's not clear whether this is good, bad or indifferent.} 46. Kd3 Kb7 47. Rh3 Rg7 48. Ke4 Rf7 49. Rh5 Kc7 50. Rf5 Re7+ {I realised that I had gone wrong} 51. Kf4 {[59']} Rg7 {[2hr 30']} 52. Rf8 Re7 53. h4 Rg7 ({Maybe} 53... Kb7 {offers more hope.}) 54. Kf5 Kb7 55. Ke6 Rg6+ {Adjourned.} 56. Rf6 {[Sealed Move]} Rg4 57. Kxd6 ({An easier win than} 57. Rf7+ Kb8 58. Rxh7) 57... Rxh4 58. Kd7 Rxb4 59. Rb6+ Ka7 60. d6 Rc4 61. Rc6 Kb7 62. Rc7+ ({and not} 62. Rxc4 $4) 62... Kb8 63. Rxc4 {Inaccurate endgame play. I should have activated the Rook. 40......xa2 seems O.K. Nigel Short had the British Expert title (BX)— held by players who achieve a B.C.F. grading of at least 200 in two consecutive lists.} 1-0

