1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. c3 Nf6 4. Bd3 {A playable system, despite its odd appearance.} Nc6 5. Bc2 Bg4 6. d3 g6 7. Be3 {Perhaps this bishop is slightly misplaced here.} Bg7 8. Nbd2 O-O 9. h3 Bd7 10. O-O {I offered a draw here. Black thought for about 35 minutes and replied...} e5 11. Rb1 Qc7 12. a3 b6 13. b4 cxb4 ({Played without hesitation, but it appears anti-positional to me.} 13... Nh5 {is better.}) 14. axb4 d5 15. Bb3 dxe4 16. dxe4 Nh5 17. Bd5 {Although I again offered a draw here, I already felt I was gaining the upper hand. Andersson also thought he had the better position.} Rac8 18. c4 Nf4 19. c5 bxc5 $2 ({Now White is clearly better.} 19... b5 $1 {would have taken c4 away from White's knight and prepared ...a5 at a later date.}) 20. bxc5 Rb8 21. Rxb8 Rxb8 22. Nc4 Ne6 23. Qd2 a5 ({Played, probably, through lack of anything constructive to do, for} 23... Ncd4 {loses to} 24. Ncxe5) 24. Ra1 Ncd4 25. Qxa5 Qxc5 26. Qxc5 Nxc5 27. Nfxe5 Nde6 28. Ra7 Be8 29. Bxc5 (29. Nxf7 {is more exact.}) 29... Nxc5 30. Nxf7 Kf8 31. Ng5 Bd7 32. Rc7 Rc8 33. Rxc8+ Bxc8 34. Nxh7+ Ke7 35. Ng5 {White should find the rest fairly easy, but this was not the case. First I had to reach the time-control at move 40.} Ba6 36. h4 Nd3 37. Nf3 Nf4 38. Ne3 Be2 39. Ne1 Bd4 40. N1c2 Bc5 41. Bc4 Nd3 42. g3 {The sealed move. From now on White, after careful manoeuvring, pushes slowly forward.} Kd6 43. Kg2 Ke5 44. f4+ Kf6 45. e5+ Kg7 46. Bd5 Nb2 47. Be4 Ba6 48. g4 Nd3 49. Kg3 Bb5 50. Ng2 Nc1 51. Nce3 Ne2+ 52. Kf2 Ba7 53. g5 Nd4 54. Kg3 Bb8 55. Ng4 Ne2+ 56. Kf3 Nd4+ 57. Kf2 Ba7 58. N2e3 Ne2 59. Kf3 Nc3 60. Bb7 Bd3 61. Nf6 Ne2 62. Nfd5 Nd4+ 63. Kg4 Bc5 64. Bc8 Be4 65. Nc3 Bf3+ 66. Kg3 Bc6 67. Bg4 Bb4 68. Ned5 Bc5 69. Ne4 (69. h5 gxh5 70. Bxh5 Nf5+ 71. Kh3 {1-0 is the finish according to Mega Database 2022. JS}) 69... Ba3 70. Nef6 {Finally. Now White cannot be stopped from obtaining three connected passed pawns.} Bc5 71. h5 gxh5 72. Bxh5 Nf5+ 73. Kh3 {The sealed move, but Andersson resigned without resuming. And, as I had drawn against Bellin before this game was resumed, this gave me my international master title. Sources: 'Nigel Short: Chess Prodigy' by David Short (Faber & Faber, 1981), p218; CHESS, February 1980, Vol.45/831-2, p78; (moves 1-42) BCM, March 1980, p120; Informator 29/332.} 1-0

