1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 d5 3. Bg2 c6 4. d3 Bg4 5. h3 Bh5 (5... Bxf3 6. Bxf3 e6 {was also quite satisfactory for Black.}) 6. g4 Bg6 7. Nh4 {White hunts down the bishop, but this consumes valuable time and allows Black a free hand in the centre.} e5 8. Nc3 Be7 9. e3 O-O $2 ({White's passive opening is com­pletely harmless, but Black reacts care­lessly. The only area in which White has an advantage is the kingside. where he controls more space, so it is folly to castle immediately on that side of the board. Black should have left his king in the centre and continued developing by} 9... Nbd7 {, leaving open the option of castling on either side.}) 10. Nxg6 hxg6 $2 (10... fxg6 {was better, but is still oblivious to the danger.}) 11. g5 Ne8 12. h4 b5 13. a3 a5 {Black tries to secure counlerplay, but White's attack grows from move to move.} 14. Qg4 Nc7 15. e4 d4 16. Ne2 Qd7 17. Qg3 {Having supported the g-pawn, White is ready for the final break­through by h5 opening the h-file.} Re8 18. Bh3 Qd8 19. h5 Bd6 20. hxg6 fxg6 21. f4 exf4 22. Bxf4 Bxf4 23. Nxf4 Qd6 24. O-O-O b4 25. Rdf1 bxa3 26. Qh4 axb2+ 27. Kb1 Qa3 {Black threatens mate in one. but White's blow lands first.} 28. Qh7+ {Source: Illustrated London News, 1 March 1984} (28. Qh7+ {After} Kxh7 {White mates by} ({while if} 28... Kf8 {or 28...Kf7 mate follows all the same after} 29. Nd5#) 29. Be6#) 1-0

